AB  - Electric transportation system is gaining greater adoption than ever due to its emission free nature and gradual implementation of on-road charging lanes. This research proposes an innovative approach to optimize dynamic wireless charging (DWC) systems by integrating a resonant inverter with a k-Nearest Neighbor (kNN) algorithm. As DWC for electric vehicles faces critical challenges due to varying distances and misalignments between transmitter and receiver coils, use of resonant inverter enhances power transfer efficiency. The kNN algorithm utilizes historical data to predict optimal coil parameters like size and turns in real-time, considering both distance fluctuations and coil misalignments. Achieving optimal efficiency for wireless power transfer (WPT) is highly challenging due to varying distance between transmitter and receiver coils, and misalignment issues. This paper proposes a hybrid approach that combines a resonant inverter circuit with a weighted-optimized kNN algorithm to optimize dynamic operating parameters (like alignment detection, frequency tuning, pad activation timing, compensation adjustment) for improved efficiency under varying conditions, while the coil design parameters (size, turns) are pre-optimized during the hardware design stage. The test system with realistic scenarios is simulated in software environment for road lane of 1 km and coil distance of 27 cm. It also includes roadside solar panel system along with boost converter supplies direct current power to resonant inverter that converts it to high frequency alternating current output. Extensive test cases are conducted on laboratory hardware setup to demonstrate significant improvements in WPT efficiency (93.5 percentage) and robust estimation of coil parameters under varying alignment scenarios.
