AB  - In recent years, Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR)-based multi-person three-dimensional (3D) pose estimation has faced significant challenges due to the sparsity and incompleteness of point cloud data, often resulting in large errors for distant or occluded targets. An attention-based, multi-frame, multi-person 3D pose estimation method for point clouds is proposed to address these issues. This method accurately predicts human 3D skeletons and improves pose estimation for occluded targets by leveraging multi-frame point cloud features to recover missing joint information caused by sparsity or incompleteness. Specifically, a lightweight 3D convolutional module enhanced with attention is designed to generate fine-grained voxel features, while a hybrid sparse–dense convolutional module effectively integrates local and global information. Furthermore, an attention-guided multi-frame regression module with learnable query embeddings directly predicts skeleton joints from fused temporal features. Experimental results on a public outdoor dataset demonstrate that our method achieves an average precision of 73.24 %, outperforming state-of-the-art baselines by 0.74 %. Notably, under severe occlusion conditions, our approach improves joint localization accuracy by more than 12 %. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed deep learning-based artificial intelligence (AI) approach for improving the accuracy and robustness of 3D human pose estimation in LiDAR-based outdoor surveillance systems, showcasing a practical application of AI in intelligent monitoring.
