AB  - Water surface object detection plays an essential role in autonomous driving on water surfaces. Compared to other object detection tasks, water surface object detection has its unique challenges, such as a wider range of variation in object scale, more background noise caused by wave and reflection, etc. In this paper, an effective information enhancement neural network with the optional localization mode is proposed to detect water surface objects more accurately. The detector collects and enhances multi-level information through an Alignment-Fusion-Enhancement network, which integrates features containing similar information in groups to avoid information drift and introduces frequency domain filters to learn the dependencies between elements in space with low complexity. An information search mechanism is designed to allocate information from the enhanced feature map to the original feature map. In addition, a detection head with an optional localization mode is designed, which chooses the appropriate localization mode for different objects. After relabeling any incorrect annotations in the Singapore Maritime dataset, a modified dataset is provided. Extensive experiments conducted on three real-world datasets demonstrate the effectiveness and efficiency of our method. The proposed detector achieved 98.6, 48.9, and 58.1 Average Precision on three datasets respectively, achieving state-of-the-art performance. Meanwhile, the special designs were all developed following the principle of lightweighting, enabling the detector to maintain the efficiency and achieve 55.2 frames per second. The excellent performance of the detector can provide strong support for autonomous navigation and obstacle avoidance of unmanned surface vehicles in tasks such as maritime surveillance and environmental monitoring.
