AB  - In forensic practice, dental age estimation is crucial for legal identification and age verification. Traditional manual methods for assessing dental development stages are time-consuming and subjective. Moreover, the stringent inclusion criteria in most previous studies have precluded cases with tooth agenesis, orthodontic interventions, etc. from receiving comprehensive systematic assessment. To address these limitations, this study developed an automated deep learning framework utilizing panoramic radiographs for dental age estimation in children and adolescents. The system was developed and validated using 5376 radiographic images from subjects aged 3.00–17.99 years, with images exhibiting tooth agenesis, orthodontic appliances, dental restorations, etc. additionally categorized as abnormal cases. Monte Carlo dropout combined with probabilistic modeling was employed to enhance predictive reliability. The system demonstrated superior performance in clinical applications: mean absolute errors (MAE) for males and females were 0.58 and 0.59 years respectively, with coefficients of determination (R2) of 0.954 and 0.959. For subjects aged 3.00–8.99 years, MAE remained within 0.5 years. When processing abnormal images, MAE differences were less than 0.05 years (p > 0.05), indicating excellent robustness. The system achieved 98 % predictive coverage within ±3 standard deviations of chronological age, providing a highly efficient and reliable solution for forensic dental age estimation.
