AB  - Nowadays, how to understand customer requirements (CRs) and design products to meet CRs in the competitive market environment is crucial for companies. Quality function deployment (QFD) is an effective customer-oriented product improvement technique, which collects the customer opinions to determine the importance ratings of CRs. However, the representation forms used by customers to express preferences on CRs may be heterogeneous and the preferences of customers may be conflicting. Thus, this paper proposes a dual-similarity based consensus reaching process (CRP) in QFD with heterogeneous linguistic preference relations (LPRs). First, considered the interference effect in quantum probability theory (QPT), the weights of customers are obtained. Then, the heterogeneous preferences of customers are converted to the relative distance matrices based on the distances between their preferences and the ideal solution. Since both the distance similarity between the customers' relative matrices and the ordinal similarity between the customers' ranking orders can reflect the similarity between the DMs' preferences from different perspectives. Two consensus levels considering the distance similarity and the ordinal similarity are defined. Subsequently, several minimum adjustment models are constructed. The validity and effectiveness of the proposed method are demonstrated by an example of live streaming channels improvement and several sensitivity and comparative analyses.
