AB  - The present analysis explores the squeezed flow of Boger fluid past two parallel disks subjected to a magnetic field. Additionally, the influence of thermal radiation, endothermic/exothermic chemical reaction, porous medium, and activation energy on the fluid flow is considered to evaluate the heat and mass transport features. The examination of Boger fluid dynamics, including chemical reactions, has considerable relevance in polymer processing, lubrication systems, reactive systems, and microfluidic devices, where precise regulation of non-Newtonian fluid behaviour is crucial. Understanding the synergistic effects of magnetic fields, thermal radiation, and auto-catalytic endothermic/exothermic chemical reactions is crucial for optimising industrial processes and improving energy efficiency in complex fluid systems. The reduced equations are solved numerically using the Runge-Kutta Fehlberg fourth-fifth order approach. Moreover, an artificial neural network driven by the Levenberg-Marquardt algorithm is designed to emulate and forecast flow behaviour with high efficiency. When raising the magnetic parameter in the suction case, the velocity profile initially decreases and then strengthens. As the magnetic parameter increases during the blowing case, the velocity profile also rises. In both endothermic and exothermic situations, the thermal profile increases with a higher radiation parameter. The concentration profile decreases as the reaction rate parameter increases for both endothermic and exothermic chemical reaction scenarios. The neural network has the lowest training errors, ranging from 1E-08 to 1E-12, indicating exceptional accuracy and predictive capability. The low relative absolute error, ranging from 1E-05 to 1E-08 for different parameters, signifies the accuracy of the proposed research.
