AB  - Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) architectures incorporating Depthwise Separable Convolution (DSC) are anticipated to maintain a high level of model accuracy while reducing spatial complexity. In contrast, their hardware architecture has received little consideration. Prior research on CNN accelerators based on DSC has commonly employed fixed computational units across different models, resulting in a compromise between power consumption, efficiency, and performance. To address this problem, three reusable parallel computing Intellectual Property cores (IPs) for computing DSC-based CNN layers (depth-wise, batch normalization, and point-wise) are designed. Furthermore, double buffering is established to balance the compute and communication time. Moreover, General Matrix Multiply (GEMM)-based computations are made for point-wise layers to fasten the computations. Additionally, the compute-intensive part of the DSC block is reduced by applying Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) rank approximation to the parameters of the point-wise layers. The accuracy and storage requirements of the proposed DSC-based CNN model are analyzed by varying the SVD-based rank approximation value. The proposed architecture demonstrates remarkable performance at 64.48 Frames Per Second (FPS), surpassing state-of-the-art designs. It achieved an impressive throughput of 288.28 Giga Operations Per Second (GOP/s) with the Xilinx ZCU104 Field-Programmable Gate Array (FPGA), highlighting its computational efficiency. Additionally, the architecture excels in energy efficiency, with a rating of 51.98 Giga Operations Per Second Per Watt (GOP/s/W), underscoring its eco-friendliness. GitHub link: https://github.com/MUNI9849/FPGA-Powered-Acceleration-of-MobileNet-V1.
