AB  - Breast cancer remains the second leading cause of cancer-related mortality among women worldwide, following lung cancer. Accurate and timely diagnosis is critical for improving patient prognosis and guiding effective treatment strategies. Despite substantial progress in developing models for breast cancer detection, many existing approaches fail to simultaneously address two key challenges: systematic feature selection and class imbalance. This study proposes a robust machine learning-based diagnostic framework capable of reliably classifying breast cancer cases as benign or malignant. To mitigate the class imbalance inherent in the dataset, the KMeans-based Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique was employed. Furthermore, a comprehensive feature selection process was conducted using Chi-square testing, Pearson correlation, Sequential Forward Feature Selection, and Sequential Backward Feature Selection to extract the most relevant features. Various machine learning classifiers were applied to these feature subsets, and the best-performing combinations were identified through comparative evaluation. The experiments utilized 70–30, 80–20, and 90–10 train-test splits for model validation. The proposed approach, when integrating Random Forest with the Sequential Backward Feature Selection method and data balancing, achieved a peak accuracy of 99.83 %, outperforming existing state-of-the-art models. The study not only offers a high-performing diagnostic model for breast cancer detection but also serves as a valuable reference for future academic research and clinical applications in medical data analytics using Artificial Intelligence.
