UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0952197625031240
KW  - Adaptive neuro-fuzzy inference system and particle swarm optimisation
KW  - Disk geometry
KW  - Motile microorganisms
KW  - Magneto-hydrodynamics
KW  - Von-Karman's swirling transport
AB  - Research background and significance of the present study
Numerous fields have found uses for von Kármán swirling flow analysis, making it an intriguing and actively pursued topic. These include biofuel production, rotating disc reactors, electric power generation systems, food processing, liquid metal pumping engines, spinning heat exchangers, and designs of multi-pore distributors, among numerous others. Rotating shafts are essential for many scientific and technical purposes, including geophysical tools, vacuum cleaners, pumps, and jet engines. There is a wide range of possible trajectories for simple-shaped objects, such as disks, and chaotic movements. The wall anisotropy slips and the concentration-induced blowing effects are included.
Research problem and novelty
An innovative study aims to examine the constant Von Karman flow originating across a stretching radiative disk, fluid flow through Darcy permeable medium that contains gyro tactics microorganisms, to forecast the performance of the heat transfer rate, the Adaptive Neuro-fuzzy Inference System and Optimisation of Particle Swarms technique are also used, as well as the consequences of radiation, magnetic fields, thermophoresis, Brownian motion, chemical reactions, and other factors.
Methodology of the present study
Using suitable transformations, the transport idea of microorganisms is represented by a non-linear partial differential equation, which is replaced by an associated system of ordinary differential equations. Further, it solves the transmuted boundary value problem with the matrix laboratory boundary value problem solver with a 5th-order collocation method.
Results and conclusion
Graphical representations of the effects of critical factors on density distributions of motile microorganisms, concentration, temperature, and velocity components are shown; fuzzy logic-based optimisation was able to predict the numerical results accurately, and supporting evidence from prior research is included.
